/-
Lean study 7 of the shape-language canon (DICTO, 19 Nov 2026), part W: the cube ∩ octahedron line, with the
truncated octahedron (a zero word) and the fair 14-face die Lucky 7 (a nonzero word) on it.

`coSet t` (1 < t < 2): the points with |x_i| ≤ 1 (cube) and |x| + |y| + |z| ≤ t (octahedron): 6 squares,
8 hexagons, 24 corners (the permutations of (±1, ±(t − 1), 0)), 36 edges.

* `co_isEdgeData`: for every 1 < t < 2 the corners, faces and edges satisfy `IsEdgeData` (study 5).
* `dehnSet_co`: **the Dehn invariant of the whole line**, read from the edges:
  dehnSet (coSet t) = 12√2 (2t − 3) ⊗ arccos(1/3).
  (24 edges square–hexagon of length √2 (t − 1) at π − arccos(1/√3), 12 edges hexagon–hexagon of length
  √2 (2 − t) at π − arccos(1/3); with 2 · arccos(1/√3) = π − arccos(1/3), part V.)
* `dehnSet_truncOct`: at t = 3/2 the solid is the truncated octahedron (corners: the permutations of
  (±1, ±1/2, 0)), and its invariant is zero.
* `dehnSet_co_ne_zero`: **every other member of the line is a nonzero word**.
* `dehnSet_luckySeven_ne_zero`: every member with 7/4 < t < 9/5 is a nonzero word; the fair 14-face die
  Lucky 7 is the member t = 1.7566…, the value of the study of 18 November 2026 (there given numerically, to
  100 digits, as a root of a polynomial of degree 6; not computed in Lean).

Which route for the truncated octahedron: its two dihedral angles arccos(−1/√3) and arccos(−1/3) are not rational
multiples of π, so study 4's rational-normal theorem does not apply; the edge reading does, and the angle relation
2 · arccos(1/√3) + arccos(1/3) = π (part V) makes the two kinds of edges cancel exactly when their lengths are
equal, t − 1 = 2 − t.

The edge data were generated by `checks/gen_edges.py` (exact linear functions of t, run on the compute host).
Lean checks the slack table by `simp`/`ring`, its signs on 1 < t < 2 by `linarith`, the finite tables by `decide`,
and the 91 pairs of faces one by one (36 edges by `mem_segment_of_tight`, 55 disjoint pairs by `linarith` on the
coordinates).

Credits: Mathlib `Finset.sum_ite`, `Finset.sum_const`, `Set.pair_eq_pair_iff`, `Real.sqrt_mul_self`; studies 1,
4, 5 and part V of this study, as named at the point of use.
No `sorry`.
-/
